  "worked_solution": "Approaching from the left, we have $x<0,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=\\sqrt{-x}+1.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}(\\sqrt{-x}+1) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{-0}+1 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApproaching from the right, we have $x>0,$ so we use the expression $f(x)={e}^{x}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}{e}^{x} \\\\  & ={e}^{0} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}f(x)=1.\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
